02:01.040 --> 02:03.280
I think it might have been about 75% in yesterday's video.

02:03.440 --> 02:08.860
But we are still overwhelmingly thinking, at least the market is, I mean, we're probably going to remain paused at the next meeting.

02:08.860 --> 02:11.100
So in life, we each get to choose, right?

02:11.180 --> 02:18.120
You can be the type of person that reacts to the headline data and gets frustrated by the tape and reacts to every knee-jerk move in the market,

02:18.400 --> 02:23.700
completely ignoring people like me that say, expect some chop games and volatility in CPI.

02:23.760 --> 02:25.040
I literally say this every month.

02:25.240 --> 02:29.640
And those types of people probably have a dining room chair in the corner of their bedroom facing the bed.

02:30.300 --> 02:30.780
Bruh.

02:34.080 --> 02:35.260
What did he say?

02:35.860 --> 02:37.800
Anyway, the point is this, right?

02:37.800 --> 02:43.520
You can be those types of people or you can have a look under the hood at what's going on really, okay?

02:43.600 --> 02:49.040
You can have a little drill down into the data and see if everything is quite as it seems or not.

02:49.260 --> 02:52.720
And so we did get a hotter than expected print solely due to shelter.

02:52.920 --> 02:53.800
No surprises there.

02:53.860 --> 02:55.120
It's always shelter causing the problem.

02:55.540 --> 03:00.040
And the Fed keeps talking about this inflation being above target for about five and a half years now.

03:00.240 --> 03:02.420
They never say what is actually above target.

03:02.660 --> 03:04.140
What they are talking about is not inflation.

03:04.140 --> 03:09.280
Those are a few flawed statistical components that don't show actual inflation.

03:09.600 --> 03:15.640
In the case of core PCE, we're talking about disrupted seasonal adjustments and a few components that misrepresent actual inflation,

03:15.800 --> 03:19.260
including trade services, portfolio management, software, legal services.

03:19.560 --> 03:23.440
And yes, those components are still misrepresented even after last month's revisions.

03:23.820 --> 03:25.000
Now check this out, okay?

03:25.000 --> 03:31.960
In the case of core CPI, the shelter components add in 3.2% year over year, which keeps showing these pie-in-the-sky numbers.

03:32.300 --> 03:38.440
Meanwhile, if you substitute for a real-time metric like ALNRI, we've been negative for over three years.

03:38.660 --> 03:40.420
Here's a visual representation of this.

03:40.560 --> 03:41.520
Blue is the shelter.

03:41.780 --> 03:46.500
It makes the inflation look significantly stickier than when you use a real-time metric,

03:46.840 --> 03:51.580
which as you can see has been significantly below target for ages.

03:51.580 --> 03:54.460
And what happens if you switch shelter for actual rent inflation?

03:54.860 --> 04:00.540
You wouldn't believe it, but core CPI with ALNRI drops to just 0.8% year over year.

04:00.700 --> 04:01.760
Is that above target?

04:01.880 --> 04:02.460
Of course not.

04:02.680 --> 04:04.380
So what is going on here?

04:04.540 --> 04:06.440
You've just got, like I said, these two types of people.

04:06.560 --> 04:09.420
One that had these knee-jerk reactions to the tape.

04:09.700 --> 04:11.060
They believe the headline data.

04:11.360 --> 04:12.660
They never think hard on it.

04:12.720 --> 04:15.120
And then there are those that actually look underneath the hood.

04:15.160 --> 04:19.520
And when you look underneath the hood, you get a completely different picture and a completely different story.

04:19.520 --> 04:24.560
